lessons.push({
id:'s2-g3-t2',batch:'07',title:'PAgP',subtitle:'Keep proprietary aggregation, trunk negotiation and silent-partner policy distinct.',level:'Intermediate → Production',minutes:50,
definition:'Port Aggregation Protocol is a Cisco-proprietary aggregation protocol supported by appropriate Cisco and licensed implementations. It helps coordinate compatible Ethernet members through PAgP exchanges. On the selected platform, desirable initiates and auto responds under ordinary negotiation with responsive PAgP-capable peers. Those names also appear in DTP, but the protocols answer different questions: PAgP forms aggregation; DTP negotiates selected switchport trunk behavior.',
purpose:'PAgP remains relevant when inspecting an installed supported network or adapting a specific lab procedure. Operators need to recognize its partner/mode observations without treating it as universal interoperability or confusing it with LACP and DTP. This lesson teaches normal responsive-peer pairing and separately identifies silent/non-silent and legacy learn-method considerations that require their exact supported context.',
mental:'An equipment-specific conversation agrees which compatible parallel connections form one logical route. Two peers speaking that protocol can initiate or answer; using an active-sounding word from another conversation does not translate it. Silence can have a separately configured meaning on a supported implementation, so a simple responsive-peer table must state its assumptions rather than claim to describe every fallback.',
components:['Supported peers: confirm PAgP feature availability and exact product/release applicability on both ends. A non-Cisco peer cannot be assumed to support it merely because Ethernet or LACP is available.','Desirable and auto: desirable starts ordinary PAgP negotiation, auto responds. Desirable/desirable and desirable/auto are selected eligible pairs; auto/auto lacks an initiator under the responsive-peer assumptions.','Compatible member set: compare actual speed/duplex, role, native/access VLAN and applicable VLAN range. A desirable setting cannot combine arbitrary links or correct an inconsistent topology.','Silent/non-silent policy: supported procedures distinguish expectations about partner traffic/protocol response. The browser excludes silent-partner behavior rather than claiming an absent exchange always determines every platform outcome.','Learning/interoperability context: legacy physical versus aggregate learning can require supported settings. On the selected Catalyst guide, command exposure does not imply its hardware performs every legacy learning behavior; avoid a generic learn-method recipe.'],
mechanism:['Identify the intended peers, member interfaces and current logical role. Confirm exact PAgP support before choosing a proprietary protocol. An installed configuration is evidence of intent, not proof that the remote device understands or participates.','Separate PAgP mode from DTP mode even when both use auto/desirable words. Inspect which command context is being discussed: channel-group aggregation and switchport trunk negotiation are different operations. A successfully negotiated trunk is not automatically an EtherChannel.','For the ordinary responsive-peer exercise, choose desirable/desirable or desirable/auto and retain compatible carrier/member/partner assumptions. Auto/auto has no initiator in this selected case. LACP active is a protocol mismatch rather than a PAgP substitute.','Review the actual supported silent/non-silent and learning behavior when applicable. A silent-server case is not represented by the responsive-peer exercise, and static on is a distinct forced method. Do not infer safe aggregation from the absence of a protocol reply.','Inspect actual PAgP neighbor, internal and counter observations, then the operational group/member flags. Read output legends and compare the intended physical map on both peers. Repeated transmitted packets alone do not prove the required members joined the correct bundle.','Verify logical VLAN/path and the original service. In an authorized isolated test, observe the intended member-loss and restoration behavior with actual policies. Retain remaining capacity, disruption and shared failure domains; PAgP negotiation does not establish application recovery by itself.'],
flow:[['Confirm supported protocol','Both peers and exact release.'],['Separate aggregation from trunking','PAgP is not DTP or LACP.'],['Review pairing and special policy','Responsive peers, member compatibility and applicable exceptions.'],['Inspect actual bundle and service','Neighbor/internal evidence, member flags and original transaction.']],
comparison:[['PAgP desirable','Initiates ordinary PAgP negotiation','Different from DTP desirable despite the same word.'],['PAgP auto','Responds under the selected protocol context','Auto/auto lacks an ordinary initiator in this exercise.'],['LACP active/passive','Different aggregation protocol','Cannot negotiate with PAgP for the same group.'],['Static on','Forces grouping without PAgP/LACP exchange','Requires deliberate compatible topology and is not a safe fallback recipe.'],['Silent/non-silent','Applicable partner-expectation policy','Excluded from this responsive-peer model; inspect exact behavior.']],
example:'A technician reads switchport mode dynamic desirable and assumes aggregation will initiate. That command concerns DTP trunk behavior; it does not establish PAgP channel participation. In the PAgP exercise the local mode is aggregation desirable and the peer is aggregation auto. Selecting LACP active on the peer demonstrates a protocol mismatch, while clearing compatible settings demonstrates another prerequisite failure. Returning to all checked assumptions yields eligibility only. No neighbor exchange, silent fallback, actual bundle or service test has been performed.',
commands:[switchcmd('show pagp 1 neighbor\nshow pagp 1 internal\nshow pagp 1 counters','Inspect supported actual group-1 PAgP partner, internal and traffic observations. Preserve the interface/peer map.','Actual observations only when run on a supporting authorized target; no synthetic Cisco transcript is supplied.'),switchcmd('show etherchannel 1 summary\nshow etherchannel 1 detail','Read actual group and member flags with their output legend.','Operational aggregation evidence, narrower than end-to-end service.'),switchcmd('show interfaces gigabitEthernet 1/0/1 switchport\nshow interfaces trunk','Inspect trunk behavior and VLAN carriage separately from PAgP aggregation.','DTP/switchport/path context cannot substitute for PAgP member evidence.')],
configuration:'The supported channel-group mode desirable selects PAgP aggregation intent; switchport dynamic desirable selects a different DTP intent. Neither line is a full two-peer deployment. Use exact role, member, partner-expectation and topology procedures with independent access and rollback. Silent/non-silent or legacy learn-method settings are context-dependent, so the lesson supplies inspections and a scoped planning exercise rather than a blind workaround configuration.',
ports:'PAgP uses link-level control exchanges on supporting members rather than a TCP/UDP application listener. User traffic, trunk tagging and discovery/control protocols remain distinct observations even when they share a physical link. The exercise sends no control frames and connects to no device. Protocol-specific frame encodings, packet timers and capture analysis are outside this selected pairing model.',
production:'Record why the actual peer environment requires or supports PAgP, including migration/interoperability requirements. A proprietary protocol choice affects partner compatibility and cannot be generalized to every server or switch. Review supported stacking/multi-device features for the exact system rather than assuming two separate devices present one aggregation partner. Change formation method through a reviewed coordinated procedure; do not mix PAgP and LACP in one intended group.',
troubleshooting:[['Symptom','A trunk is present but expected PAgP aggregation/service is absent.'],['Hypotheses','DTP/PAgP confusion, no ordinary initiator, unsupported peer, incompatible members, special-policy or later path failure.'],['Test','Identify command context, both aggregation modes, actual PAgP neighbor/internal state and bundle flags.'],['Observation','The authored checker distinguishes mode mismatch or prerequisite failure from mere eligibility.'],['Conclusion','A trunk or active-sounding word is not evidence of the required aggregation protocol.'],['Repair and verify','Correct the demonstrated supported target fault, inspect actual group/member and VLAN path, then repeat the original service and permitted loss test.']],
security:'Proprietary negotiation does not authenticate a partner or encrypt user traffic. Retain intended VLAN and access boundaries and review any policy that permits forwarding without the expected partner response. A forced static bundle or unreviewed silent configuration can change availability and loop exposure. Restrict operational output sharing and avoid turning a basic diagnosis into indiscriminate debugging on a production switch.',
performance:'PAgP negotiation and data-plane distribution are distinct. Desirable is not a promise of more payload capacity, and protocol counters do not measure application throughput. Count actual selected forwarding members, inspect traffic assignment and measure the defined workload/degraded state. Legacy learning guidance must match the peer and platform; exposed commands alone do not prove a hardware behavior. This model implements no hash, timers or silent recovery.',
mistakes:['Confusing DTP desirable with PAgP desirable.','Assuming every Ethernet or LACP peer supports PAgP.','Generalizing the responsive-peer auto/auto result to every silent policy.','Forcing on to conceal inconsistent grouping.','Calling sent control counters a complete bundle/service test.'],interactive:'pagp-pair',
lab:{objective:'Separate PAgP responsive-peer eligibility from DTP, LACP and platform-specific silent/learning behavior.',requirements:'Browser checker only. Actual PAgP needs an authorized supported peer pair and exact release/topology procedure.',topology:['PAgP-capable local intent','Selected peer protocol/mode','Compatible mapped members','Separate trunk/path and service verification'],steps:['Compare desirable/auto, desirable/desirable and auto/auto under the stated responsive-peer assumptions.','Choose each LACP peer mode and explain why active-sounding intent cannot negotiate PAgP.','Clear each prerequisite and distinguish mode eligibility from usable carrier, settings and partner topology.','Reset and identify actual neighbor/internal/member/path/service evidence still pending.','Write separate notes for PAgP aggregation, DTP trunking and any supported silent/non-silent or legacy learning requirement.','Plan an authorized baseline, scoped member-loss test and restoration record without claiming execution.'],verification:'The independent 128-state checker covers all four protocol modes and three explicit boolean assumptions. Browser tests cover native selection, reset, quizzes, original adjacency, mobile and disk operation. Special silent/standalone policy, real protocol/member states and Cisco execution are excluded.',troubleshooting:'Use actual supported observations for silent or legacy-learning cases; do not use this table to fabricate device output or predict every nonbundled state.',challenge:'The peer supports LACP but not PAgP. Why does changing only its LACP active/passive role fail to fix a local PAgP desirable configuration?',cleanup:'Reset browser assumptions. No configuration, discovery or link state changed; actual maintenance cleanup restores reviewed state and repeats service verification.'},
quick:[q('Beginner','Initiation','Which PAgP mode initiates ordinary responsive-peer negotiation?','Desirable.','Auto responds in that selected context.'),q('Intermediate','Context','Does DTP desirable establish PAgP aggregation?','No.','The two command contexts serve different purposes.'),q('Advanced','Scope','Does the model implement PAgP silent-partner behavior?','No.','It explicitly assumes responsive protocol-capable peers.')],
exams:[q('Beginner','Pairing','Which selected pair has an ordinary PAgP initiator?','Desirable / auto.','Both use PAgP and one initiates.',['Desirable / auto.','Auto / auto under the responsive-peer assumptions.','LACP active / PAgP auto.','LACP passive / PAgP desirable.']),q('Intermediate','Support','Can a peer be assumed to support PAgP because it supports Ethernet?','No.','Verify exact proprietary-protocol support.'),q('Troubleshooting','DTP','A port uses DTP dynamic desirable. Does that establish PAgP member participation?','No.','Inspect aggregation separately from trunk negotiation.'),q('Design','Protocol boundary','Can PAgP and LACP negotiate one intended group with each other?','No.','Choose a compatible supported formation method.'),q('Security','Protection','Does proprietary negotiation encrypt the user payload?','No.','Aggregation and payload protection are separate.'),q("Performance","Command context","A transcript contains switchport mode dynamic desirable but no PAgP member evidence. Which function is represented and which observations establish aggregation separately?","The command represents DTP trunk intent; inspect actual PAgP channel-group, neighbor/internal and bundle/member state separately.","The same desirable word belongs to distinct control functions."),q('Advanced','Silent scope','Why is auto/auto described under responsive-peer assumptions?','Silent-partner policy is a separate supported behavior excluded from the checker.','Do not generalize the simple table to all fallback cases.'),q('Intermediate','Legacy learning','Does an exposed legacy learn-method command prove the selected hardware uses every legacy learning behavior?','No.','Consult exact hardware and interoperability guidance.'),q("Troubleshooting","Peer protocol mismatch","Local PAgP desirable faces a peer supporting LACP only. What supported planning correction should precede another test?","Coordinate a mutually supported formation method and compatible intended member topology on both ends.","Changing only LACP active/passive cannot make it negotiate PAgP; do not force static on to conceal the mismatch."),q('Design','Verification','What evidence must follow eligible checker output?','Actual partner/member/logical-path and original service observations.','The browser checks selected prerequisites rather than execution.')],
glossary:{'PAgP':'Port Aggregation Protocol.','Desirable':'Selected PAgP initiating mode; distinct from the same word in DTP.','Auto':'Selected PAgP responding mode under stated normal negotiation assumptions.','Silent partner':'Applicable supported partner-expectation context requiring exact procedure.'},prerequisites:['s2-g3-t0','s2-g2-t3'],related:['s2-g3-t1','s2-g2-t2'],sources:[aggregationSources.guide,aggregationSources.diagnosis,vlanSources.trunks]
});

